1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,370
If you've ever felt like a fraud...

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:05,120
like you only got here by luck, and any day

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,782
now someone will find out...

4
00:00:06,822 --> 00:00:09,276
here's what the research actually says.

5
00:00:09,316 --> 00:00:12,154
It's common, in men and women, from teenagers

6
00:00:12,194 --> 00:00:13,689
to senior professionals.

7
00:00:13,729 --> 00:00:16,502
And in one study, people with these thoughts

8
00:00:16,542 --> 00:00:18,421
were rated better with others...

9
00:00:18,461 --> 00:00:19,450
not worse.

10
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,322
Picture it. You get the good news.

11
00:00:23,362 --> 00:00:26,774
The promotion, the acceptance letter, the

12
00:00:26,814 --> 00:00:28,963
compliment from your boss.

13
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:31,657
And for one second you're happy...

14
00:00:31,697 --> 00:00:34,014
and then a quiet voice says

15
00:00:34,054 --> 00:00:35,698
they made a mistake.

16
00:00:35,738 --> 00:00:37,298
You just got lucky.

17
00:00:37,338 --> 00:00:40,466
Wait until they see the real you.

18
00:00:41,516 --> 00:00:43,183
So where does that voice come from?

19
00:00:43,223 --> 00:00:44,938
Why doesn't success make it go away?

20
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:46,986
Is that famous seventy percent number even

21
00:00:47,026 --> 00:00:49,521
real? And what can you actually do about it?

22
00:00:55,371 --> 00:00:57,385
In nineteen seventy eight, two psychologists

23
00:00:57,425 --> 00:00:59,392
at Georgia State University, Pauline Clance

24
00:00:59,432 --> 00:01:01,306
and Suzanne Imes, published a paper about

25
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:03,657
something they kept hearing in their work.

26
00:01:04,707 --> 00:01:07,048
They described more than a hundred and fifty

27
00:01:07,088 --> 00:01:08,347
highly successful women.

28
00:01:08,387 --> 00:01:10,837
Women with advanced degrees, with honors, with

29
00:01:10,877 --> 00:01:13,175
respected jobs and plenty of praise.

30
00:01:14,225 --> 00:01:17,211
And yet these women said, again and again

31
00:01:17,251 --> 00:01:19,517
that they weren't really bright.

32
00:01:19,557 --> 00:01:21,679
That they had fooled everyone.

33
00:01:21,719 --> 00:01:24,921
That their success was a mistake, or luck, or

34
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:27,299
other people overestimating them...

35
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:29,202
anything but ability.

36
00:01:30,252 --> 00:01:32,044
They gave it a name

37
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:34,243
the impostor phenomenon.

38
00:01:34,283 --> 00:01:37,175
Notice the word. Not a syndrome.

39
00:01:37,215 --> 00:01:39,832
Not a disorder. A phenomenon...

40
00:01:39,872 --> 00:01:43,795
an experience that lots of people have.

41
00:01:44,845 --> 00:01:46,172
That still matters today.

42
00:01:46,212 --> 00:01:48,196
Impostor syndrome is not a recognized

43
00:01:48,236 --> 00:01:49,400
psychiatric diagnosis.

44
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:51,861
You won't find it in the manual psychiatrists

45
00:01:51,901 --> 00:01:54,323
use. It's a pattern of thoughts and feelings

46
00:01:54,363 --> 00:01:55,533
not an illness.

47
00:01:56,583 --> 00:01:59,135
A few years later, Clance built a twenty item

48
00:01:59,175 --> 00:02:00,748
questionnaire to measure it

49
00:02:00,788 --> 00:02:02,879
the Clance Impostor Phenomenon Scale.

50
00:02:02,919 --> 00:02:05,529
It became the most used tool in this research.

51
00:02:05,569 --> 00:02:08,006
So when you read a statistic about impostor

52
00:02:08,046 --> 00:02:10,540
feelings, it usually comes from a score on a

53
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:12,082
scale like this one.

54
00:02:13,132 --> 00:02:14,465
One more honest point.

55
00:02:14,505 --> 00:02:16,835
That first paper was built on clinical

56
00:02:16,875 --> 00:02:19,456
observations, not a controlled experiment.

57
00:02:19,496 --> 00:02:20,953
It was a starting point.

58
00:02:20,993 --> 00:02:23,574
The real test came later, when researchers

59
00:02:23,614 --> 00:02:25,274
started measuring it.

60
00:02:26,324 --> 00:02:28,941
And when they did, the first big surprise was

61
00:02:28,981 --> 00:02:29,980
who has it.

62
00:02:33,630 --> 00:02:35,924
In twenty twenty, a team led by Dena Bravata

63
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:38,151
at Stanford published a systematic review.

64
00:02:38,191 --> 00:02:40,485
They pulled together sixty two studies, with

65
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:42,731
more than fourteen thousand people.

66
00:02:43,781 --> 00:02:46,206
The first finding: impostor feelings were

67
00:02:46,246 --> 00:02:47,949
common in both men and women.

68
00:02:47,989 --> 00:02:50,714
The original paper was about women, and Clance

69
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:53,299
later said she had certainly seen it in men

70
00:02:53,339 --> 00:02:55,944
too. In the review, some studies found women

71
00:02:55,984 --> 00:02:57,146
reported more of it...

72
00:02:57,186 --> 00:03:00,241
and about as many found no difference at all.

73
00:03:01,291 --> 00:03:02,967
They found it across ages, too.

74
00:03:03,007 --> 00:03:05,347
From teenagers in school, to people late in

75
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:07,616
their careers. Nursing students, doctors

76
00:03:07,656 --> 00:03:09,387
teachers, managers, accountants.

77
00:03:09,427 --> 00:03:11,933
In several studies, rates were especially high

78
00:03:11,973 --> 00:03:13,538
among ethnic minority groups.

79
00:03:13,578 --> 00:03:16,087
Experience alone doesn't switch it off.

80
00:03:17,137 --> 00:03:19,901
Now, about that number you've probably seen.

81
00:03:19,941 --> 00:03:22,642
Seventy percent of people will feel like an

82
00:03:22,682 --> 00:03:24,108
impostor at some point.

83
00:03:24,148 --> 00:03:26,785
It's everywhere. In articles, in talks, on

84
00:03:26,825 --> 00:03:27,685
posters.

85
00:03:28,735 --> 00:03:31,580
Here's the thing. The review didn't find one

86
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:34,464
number. Depending on the study, rates ranged

87
00:03:34,504 --> 00:03:37,349
from nine percent… all the way to eighty two

88
00:03:37,389 --> 00:03:38,263
percent.

89
00:03:39,313 --> 00:03:42,009
Why so wide? Mostly because of how it was

90
00:03:42,049 --> 00:03:45,012
measured. Different questionnaires, different

91
00:03:45,052 --> 00:03:46,547
cutoffs for who counts.

92
00:03:46,587 --> 00:03:49,873
Move the line, and the number moves with it.

93
00:03:50,923 --> 00:03:52,525
That doesn't mean it's rare.

94
00:03:52,565 --> 00:03:55,045
Even the low end, nine percent, is a lot of

95
00:03:55,085 --> 00:03:57,449
people. It means the exact number is much

96
00:03:57,489 --> 00:03:59,715
softer than the posters suggest.

97
00:04:00,765 --> 00:04:02,638
And that seventy percent figure?

98
00:04:02,678 --> 00:04:05,209
It's usually traced to a two thousand seven

99
00:04:05,249 --> 00:04:07,541
newspaper article quoting a researcher.

100
00:04:07,581 --> 00:04:10,231
The original study behind it is hard to find.

101
00:04:10,271 --> 00:04:11,965
So treat it as a rough guess...

102
00:04:12,005 --> 00:04:13,013
not a fact.

103
00:04:14,063 --> 00:04:16,740
What the review does show clearly is this.

104
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:19,392
Impostor feelings often show up alongside

105
00:04:19,432 --> 00:04:22,303
anxiety and depression, and they're linked to

106
00:04:22,343 --> 00:04:24,567
burnout and lower job satisfaction.

107
00:04:24,607 --> 00:04:26,572
So it's not just a funny quirk.

108
00:04:26,612 --> 00:04:28,450
It can weigh on people.

109
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:31,484
Which raises the obvious question.

110
00:04:31,524 --> 00:04:33,330
If these people are succeeding...

111
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:35,983
why doesn't the success convince them?

112
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:42,043
Clance later described what she called the

113
00:04:42,083 --> 00:04:44,318
impostor cycle. And once you see it, it

114
00:04:44,358 --> 00:04:45,583
explains a lot.

115
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:48,160
It starts with a task.

116
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,937
A presentation, an exam, a new project.

117
00:04:50,977 --> 00:04:53,359
And with it comes a wave of doubt

118
00:04:53,399 --> 00:04:56,526
what if this is the time they find out?

119
00:04:57,576 --> 00:05:00,523
From there, people tend to go one of two ways.

120
00:05:00,563 --> 00:05:03,120
Some over prepare. They work three times

121
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:06,041
harder than they need to, checking everything

122
00:05:06,081 --> 00:05:07,470
again and again.

123
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:10,993
Others put it off… and then rush at the last

124
00:05:11,033 --> 00:05:11,783
minute.

125
00:05:12,833 --> 00:05:14,495
Then the task goes well.

126
00:05:14,535 --> 00:05:17,687
Relief! But watch what happens to the credit.

127
00:05:17,727 --> 00:05:20,028
The one who over prepared thinks

128
00:05:20,068 --> 00:05:22,866
it only worked because I worked so hard.

129
00:05:22,906 --> 00:05:24,710
The procrastinator thinks

130
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:25,951
I got lucky.

131
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:29,310
Imagine someone who spent a whole week

132
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:31,041
preparing a ten minute talk.

133
00:05:31,081 --> 00:05:32,710
Her boss says it was great.

134
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,554
What she hears is: good thing I spent all week

135
00:05:35,594 --> 00:05:37,903
on it. Next time, I'll need two weeks.

136
00:05:37,943 --> 00:05:39,819
The praise lands on the effort...

137
00:05:39,859 --> 00:05:41,013
never on her.

138
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:45,167
Either way, the success never gets filed under

139
00:05:45,207 --> 00:05:48,311
ability. So the next task starts with the same

140
00:05:48,351 --> 00:05:50,977
doubt. Success doesn't break the cycle.

141
00:05:51,017 --> 00:05:52,187
It feeds it.

142
00:05:53,237 --> 00:05:56,002
It's also why more success can make it louder.

143
00:05:56,042 --> 00:05:58,686
A promotion means new people, higher stakes...

144
00:05:58,726 --> 00:06:01,454
and a brand new chance to be found out.

145
00:06:02,504 --> 00:06:03,978
There's a second trap.

146
00:06:04,018 --> 00:06:07,006
You can see your own doubts from the inside...

147
00:06:07,046 --> 00:06:09,621
but everyone else only shows you their

148
00:06:09,661 --> 00:06:10,561
outside.

149
00:06:11,611 --> 00:06:13,766
In twenty eleven, a team led by Alexander

150
00:06:13,806 --> 00:06:16,228
Jordan at Stanford asked people about everyday

151
00:06:16,268 --> 00:06:17,620
experiences, good and bad.

152
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:20,029
People said their negative feelings were more

153
00:06:20,069 --> 00:06:22,132
hidden than their positive ones.

154
00:06:23,182 --> 00:06:25,922
And they underestimated how often their peers

155
00:06:25,962 --> 00:06:27,528
went through hard moments...

156
00:06:27,568 --> 00:06:29,135
even peers they knew well.

157
00:06:29,175 --> 00:06:31,791
The people who underestimated the most also

158
00:06:31,831 --> 00:06:33,664
tended to feel lonelier.

159
00:06:34,714 --> 00:06:37,318
So you compare your messy inside with everyone

160
00:06:37,358 --> 00:06:38,697
else's polished outside.

161
00:06:38,737 --> 00:06:41,731
Of course you come out looking like the fraud.

162
00:06:45,381 --> 00:06:48,299
Now for the twist. In twenty twenty two

163
00:06:48,339 --> 00:06:51,404
Basima Tewfik, at M I T, asked a different

164
00:06:51,444 --> 00:06:54,657
question. What do impostor thoughts actually

165
00:06:54,697 --> 00:06:56,674
do to how people work?

166
00:06:57,724 --> 00:06:59,591
She started at an investment firm.

167
00:06:59,631 --> 00:07:02,116
Employees who reported more impostor thoughts

168
00:07:02,156 --> 00:07:04,641
were later rated by their supervisors as more

169
00:07:04,681 --> 00:07:06,212
effective with other people...

170
00:07:06,252 --> 00:07:07,892
and no less productive.

171
00:07:08,942 --> 00:07:10,974
Then she went to doctors in training, and

172
00:07:11,014 --> 00:07:12,995
filmed their appointments with patients.

173
00:07:13,035 --> 00:07:15,269
Patients rated the doctors with more impostor

174
00:07:15,309 --> 00:07:17,593
thoughts as more empathetic, better listeners

175
00:07:17,633 --> 00:07:19,903
and better at drawing out information.

176
00:07:20,953 --> 00:07:23,111
On the video, those doctors made more eye

177
00:07:23,151 --> 00:07:24,505
contact. They nodded more.

178
00:07:24,545 --> 00:07:26,328
They used more open hand gestures.

179
00:07:26,368 --> 00:07:28,633
They were paying attention to the person in

180
00:07:28,673 --> 00:07:29,774
front of them.

181
00:07:30,824 --> 00:07:33,247
Her explanation: when you feel you might not

182
00:07:33,287 --> 00:07:35,654
be enough, you turn your attention outward.

183
00:07:35,694 --> 00:07:36,941
You ask more questions.

184
00:07:36,981 --> 00:07:39,460
You listen harder. In two experiments, people

185
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,923
who were led to have impostor thoughts asked

186
00:07:41,963 --> 00:07:43,433
more questions, too.

187
00:07:44,483 --> 00:07:47,164
But she is clear. This is not a reason to want

188
00:07:47,204 --> 00:07:49,886
these thoughts. She also found they lower self

189
00:07:49,926 --> 00:07:52,489
esteem. And there are far better ways to get

190
00:07:52,529 --> 00:07:54,915
good with people that don't cost you your

191
00:07:54,955 --> 00:07:56,133
peace of mind.

192
00:07:57,183 --> 00:08:00,029
So the takeaway isn't that impostor feelings

193
00:08:00,069 --> 00:08:01,341
are good. It's this.

194
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:03,177
The feeling is not evidence.

195
00:08:03,217 --> 00:08:04,555
Feeling like a fraud...

196
00:08:04,595 --> 00:08:07,896
and being one… are two very different things.

197
00:08:08,946 --> 00:08:10,283
And that's the strange part.

198
00:08:10,323 --> 00:08:12,348
The very feeling that tells you you're not

199
00:08:12,388 --> 00:08:14,413
good enough can make you the person in the

200
00:08:14,453 --> 00:08:16,576
room who pays the most attention to everyone

201
00:08:16,616 --> 00:08:17,212
else.

202
00:08:20,862 --> 00:08:23,126
So what helps? First, an honest note.

203
00:08:23,166 --> 00:08:25,804
The twenty twenty review found no published

204
00:08:25,844 --> 00:08:28,295
studies testing a treatment for impostor

205
00:08:28,335 --> 00:08:31,035
feelings. So these are ideas grounded in the

206
00:08:31,075 --> 00:08:32,733
research… not a cure.

207
00:08:33,783 --> 00:08:35,892
One. Call it a thought, not a fact.

208
00:08:35,932 --> 00:08:38,715
The M I T study talks about impostor thoughts

209
00:08:38,755 --> 00:08:40,741
on purpose. Thoughts come and go.

210
00:08:40,781 --> 00:08:43,319
When the voice says you got lucky, you can

211
00:08:43,359 --> 00:08:45,775
notice it: there's that impostor thought

212
00:08:45,815 --> 00:08:46,533
again.

213
00:08:47,583 --> 00:08:50,182
Two. Say it out loud to someone you trust.

214
00:08:50,222 --> 00:08:52,632
Clance has said that learning about the

215
00:08:52,672 --> 00:08:55,460
experience, and talking about it with others

216
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,093
helps people recognize it.

217
00:08:57,133 --> 00:08:59,292
And remember the Stanford research.

218
00:08:59,332 --> 00:09:01,617
The title of that paper says it best

219
00:09:01,657 --> 00:09:04,256
misery has more company than people think.

220
00:09:04,296 --> 00:09:06,392
There's a good chance they'll say...

221
00:09:06,432 --> 00:09:07,222
me too.

222
00:09:08,272 --> 00:09:10,792
Three. Break the cycle at the credit step.

223
00:09:10,832 --> 00:09:13,596
After something goes well, write down what you

224
00:09:13,636 --> 00:09:15,607
actually did. The skill you used.

225
00:09:15,647 --> 00:09:16,948
The decision you made.

226
00:09:16,988 --> 00:09:18,107
Not just the hours...

227
00:09:18,147 --> 00:09:19,448
and not just the luck.

228
00:09:19,488 --> 00:09:22,130
Keep it somewhere you'll see it, like a note

229
00:09:22,170 --> 00:09:24,812
on your phone. Over time, you build a record

230
00:09:24,852 --> 00:09:27,274
that's a lot harder to argue with.

231
00:09:28,324 --> 00:09:30,126
And keep the bigger picture in mind.

232
00:09:30,166 --> 00:09:32,121
Researchers like Sanne Feenstra and her

233
00:09:32,161 --> 00:09:34,423
colleagues argue that the environment matters

234
00:09:34,463 --> 00:09:36,777
too. Feeling out of place can come from places

235
00:09:36,817 --> 00:09:38,260
that signal you don't belong.

236
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:40,409
It's not only something to fix inside your

237
00:09:40,449 --> 00:09:41,055
head.

238
00:09:42,105 --> 00:09:44,566
And if these feelings come with anxiety or low

239
00:09:44,606 --> 00:09:46,850
mood that won't lift, that's worth talking

240
00:09:46,890 --> 00:09:49,242
about with a doctor or a licensed therapist.

241
00:09:49,282 --> 00:09:50,276
That's not failing.

242
00:09:50,316 --> 00:09:52,188
That's using the right tool.

243
00:09:53,238 --> 00:09:55,069
So, back to that moment.

244
00:09:55,109 --> 00:09:56,784
The good news arrives...

245
00:09:56,824 --> 00:09:58,733
and the quiet voice says

246
00:09:58,773 --> 00:10:00,682
they made a mistake.

247
00:10:01,732 --> 00:10:03,741
Now you know more about that voice.

248
00:10:03,781 --> 00:10:05,732
It's common. It's not a diagnosis.

249
00:10:05,772 --> 00:10:07,489
It doesn't listen to evidence...

250
00:10:07,529 --> 00:10:09,948
and it is not the same thing as the truth.

251
00:10:09,988 --> 00:10:11,822
You probably didn't fool anyone.

252
00:10:11,862 --> 00:10:14,027
You probably just did the work.

253
00:10:15,077 --> 00:10:16,801
Here's my question for you.

254
00:10:16,841 --> 00:10:19,807
What's one success you still secretly put down

255
00:10:19,847 --> 00:10:21,310
to luck? Tell me below.

256
00:10:21,350 --> 00:10:24,251
And if you like psychology with the receipts

257
00:10:24,291 --> 00:10:25,294
subscribe.
